Major Trauma AUC-039
Acute Urgent Care
High-energy mechanism, Hypotension/Shock, Altered GCS, Multi-system injury

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-039
Reason for Visit: Severe, high-speed motor vehicle collision into a tree in front of a rural residence, resulting in an unconscious and severely injured driver.
Location: Private residence (Front yard/driveway)
Present at Visit: Patient and bystanders
Urgency Justification: I witnessed/was flagged down for a high-speed single-vehicle accident where a car struck a large tree. The driver was entrapped, unconscious, and bleeding heavily. This is a catastrophic, multi-system major trauma. Immediate, on-the-spot clinical triage, airway management, and coordination of a massive emergency response (Helicopter/Intensive Care Paramedics) were absolutely vital, as relying purely on standard rural response times without a medical first responder would guarantee death from hemorrhagic shock or airway compromise.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Bystander reports] He hit the tree going at least 100km/h, he isn't waking up, and there's blood everywhere!
History of Present Illness: The patient was the unrestrained driver in a high-energy mechanism of injury (motor vehicle vs. tree at high velocity) occurring 5 minutes ago. The patient has an altered level of consciousness and has not regained full awareness since the impact. Bystanders report the patient was initially making snoring noises but is now taking shallow, irregular breaths. The patient exhibits visible severe external haemorrhage from a large scalp laceration and an obvious major deformity of the left femur. The steering wheel is significantly deformed, indicating massive blunt force trauma to the torso.
Functional Status: Unconscious, entrapped in the driver's seat.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening injuries; vehicle is currently stable but leaking fluids.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 70/40 | HR: 135 | Temp: 35.8 | RR: 32 | SpO2: 88% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Patient is trapped in the vehicle, covered in blood, profoundly pale, and unresponsive to verbal commands.
Physical Exam: Primary survey reveals an altered level of consciousness (GCS 6: E1, V2, M3), indicating a severe traumatic brain injury or profound shock. The airway is partially compromised by blood. Respiratory exam notes tachypnoea and asymmetrical, paradoxical chest wall movement on the left, highly suspicious for a flail chest and underlying pulmonary contusion/pneumothorax. Cardiovascular exam reveals severe tachycardia and profound hypotension, indicating Class IV haemorrhagic shock. Pelvic rock reveals gross pelvic instability, a source of massive concealed haemorrhage. Expected hospital investigations include a whole-body trauma CT scan and a FAST ultrasound demonstrating solid organ injury and massive internal bleeding.
Environmental Observations: Vehicle airbags deployed; severe intrusion into the driver's footwell.
Medication Reconciliation: Unknown in the acute trauma setting.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Major Trauma - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Isolated Traumatic Brain Injury - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole cause of the vital sign derangement; the profound hypotension indicates massive systemic bleeding (pelvis/chest/femur), as isolated head injuries typically cause hypertension (Cushing's reflex), not shock.

Medical Event causing crash (e.g., Myocardial Infarction) - Ruled out/unlikely to be the current primary life threat; regardless of why the crash happened, the patient is dying from the mechanical trauma (shock, flail chest).

Simple Concussion - Ruled out/unlikely due to the GCS of 6, profound shock, and obvious multi-system deformities resulting from a high-velocity impact.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the devastating tetrad of major trauma: a high-energy mechanism, profound haemorrhagic shock, an altered GCS, and multi-system injury (flail chest, unstable pelvis, open femur fracture). The patient is dying rapidly from massive uncontained internal and external bleeding combined with hypoxia. My immediate medical presence allowed for basic life-saving interventions—airway positioning with c-spine control and application of a pressure dressing—while critically upgrading the 000 response to include an aeromedical trauma team. Immediate extrication and damage control surgery are the only viable definitive treatments.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed a jaw thrust to open the airway while maintaining manual in-line cervical spine stabilization. Directed a bystander to apply continuous, hard direct pressure to the massive scalp laceration. Upgraded 000 call to Priority 1 Aeromedical / Intensive Care Paramedic response, explicitly requesting blood products on scene. Rapidly applied a makeshift pelvic binder (using a folded sheet) around the greater trochanters to reduce pelvic volume and slow internal bleeding.
Positive Behavioral Change: (N/A - Profoundly unconscious trauma patient).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Helicopter Emergency Medical Service (HEMS) transport to a Level 1 Trauma Center.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to HEMS and extrication teams.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Maintain manual airway and c-spine control. If the patient loses their pulse, extricate immediately using a rapid takedown technique to the ground and commence CPR, though survival from traumatic cardiac arrest is exceptionally poor.

